  "title": "Distributed Transaction Challenges",
  "description": "Understand ACID properties in distributed systems.",
  "order": 1,
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "estimatedMinutes": 18,
  "tags": ["distributed-transactions", "acid", "challenges"],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Distributed transactions span multiple services, each with its own database. Maintaining ACID properties across services is hard."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Key Concepts"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "key-terms",
      "data": {
        "terms": [
          {
            "term": "ACID",
            "definition": "Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability."
          },
          {
            "term": "Atomicity",
            "definition": "All operations succeed or none do."
          },
          {
            "term": "Consistency",
            "definition": "Data moves from one valid state to another."
          },
          {
            "term": "Isolation",
            "definition": "Concurrent transactions do not interfere."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "The Problem"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "In a monolith, a single database transaction ensures ACID. In microservices, each service has its own database. A business transaction spans multiple services and databases."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Challenges"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "No shared transaction across databases",
          "Network failures can leave partial state",
          "Maintaining consistency is hard",
          "Isolation across services is impossible"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Solutions"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "data": {
        "headers": ["Pattern", "Consistency", "Complexity"],
        "rows": [
          ["2PC", "Strong", "High"],
          ["Saga", "Eventual", "Medium"],
          ["TCC", "Eventual", "High"],
          ["Outbox", "Eventual", "Medium"]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "BASE vs ACID"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Distributed systems often use BASE (Basically Available, Soft state, Eventually consistent) instead of ACID. This trades strong consistency for availability."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Architecture"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
